00:02:11.264 --> 00:02:11.898
And, Mike,

00:02:11.898 --> 00:02:13.566
I mean, a terrifying discovery, right?

00:02:13.566 --> 00:02:15.468
Another airline, right,

00:02:15.468 --> 00:02:17.003
that had taken the planes out of service

00:02:17.003 --> 00:02:18.271
after this incident, sort of

00:02:18.271 --> 00:02:19.873
out of an abundance of caution.

00:02:19.873 --> 00:02:21.841
And you often expect in these situations,

00:02:21.841 --> 00:02:22.642
take it out of service.

00:02:22.642 --> 00:02:23.643
Others, no problem.

00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:25.211
I was just too cautious.

00:02:25.211 --> 00:02:27.080
Now they're finding loose bolts on

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:28.081
multiple door plugs.

00:02:28.081 --> 00:02:30.683
What more do you know?

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:31.384
Well, we know.

00:02:31.384 --> 00:02:32.685
Thanks for the reporting from our great

00:02:32.685 --> 00:02:33.453
and esteemed colleague,

00:02:33.453 --> 00:02:34.154
Pete Montini,

00:02:34.154 --> 00:02:34.687
Erin, that

00:02:34.687 --> 00:02:36.656
in order to have these findings

00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:37.891
